.Dd November 3, 2026
.Dt SEQ 1
.Os ABSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm seq
.Nd generate a numeric sequence
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl w
.Op Fl s Ar separator
.Op Fl t Ar terminator
.Op Fl f Ar format
.Op Ar first Op Ar increment
.Ar last
.Sh DESCRIPTION
Print numbers from first through last, advancing by increment while
within the selected endpoint.
With one number, first and increment default to 1; with two, increment
defaults to 1.
A descending sequence requires a negative increment.
Floating-point operands are accepted; this is not a general exact-decimal
calculator.
Zero increments and malformed numbers are refused.
.Pp
The default separator and final terminator are newlines.
.Fl s Ar separator
changes only the separator between numbers.
.Fl t Ar terminator
changes the final terminator.
.Fl w
pads numbers to equal width with leading zeroes.
.Fl f Ar format
selects the upstream printf-style numeric format; it cannot be combined
with equal-width output.
No input stream or filesystem object is read or changed.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
0 on successful generation or a broken output pipe.
On ABSD the pinned utility diagnoses a broken pipe on standard error but
returns success; its Unix-only ignored-SIGPIPE status adjustment is absent.
Invalid arguments and other output failures return nonzero status and
are diagnosed on standard error.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Bd -literal -offset indent
seq 3
seq -s , 1 2 7
.Ed
.Sh ABSD PROFILE
This is the unmodified uutils 0.12.0 utility pinned by the platform build.
The reference describes selected source-supported options; use
.Fl Fl help
for the complete upstream interface.
File access uses the process's ABSD filesystem view, output uses its
inherited stream, and the command acquires no additional authority.
There are no native Unix processes, signals or permission bits underneath
this port.
The command matrix exercises the example forms below; other options
listed here are source-checked, not separately guest-qualified.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr printf 1 ,
.Xr intro 1
